## Test Code Coverage for .NET

Test code coverage is a measure used to see how much of your source code is exercised by your tests. It can help identify sections of the code-base that aren't being covered and might need additional tests. 

### Code coverage tools

There are several tools available that provide code coverage, which can be used to check the amount of test coverage for a particular software program. These include:

- [dotCover](https://www.jetbrains.com/dotcover/) by JetBrains
- [PartCover](https://www.partcover.com/) by [Snyk](https://snyk.io/)
- [NDepend](https://www.ndepend.com/) by NDepend
- [Coverity](https://www.coverity.com/)
- [NCover](http://www.ncover.com/) and [PartCover](https://www.partcover.com/)
- [Visual Studio](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/using-code-coverage-to-determine-how-much-code-is-being-tested)  Code coverage capability
- [OpenCover](http://www.opencover.org/) by [Scitools](https://www.scitools.net/)

## .NET Core Testing - XUnit

XUnit is a free, open-source Test Framework for the .NET Framework. It is similar to NUnit, NUnit Lite, and MSTest. XUnit was developed by the original developer of NUnit v2, Kevin Boos and carries the same "write once, run anywhere" philosophy. 

Get started with XUnit testing.

1. First, you need to install the xUnit.net v2.4.1. The package manager console can be used to install the package as follows:

   ```sh
   PM> Install-Package xunit
   ```

2. Create a new Unit test class by right-clicking your Project and selecting Add -> New Item -> Class, naming the class as needed and ensuring it ends with `Tests` (e.g., `MyClassTests`).

3. Import the Xunit framework to your test class. 

    ```csharp
    using Xunit;
    ```

4. You can now write your test methods, each of which must start with the word 'Test'.

   ```csharp
   public class MyClassTests
   {
       [Fact]
       public void MyTestMethod()
       {
           // Arrange
           var classUnderTest = new MyClass();

           // Act
           var result = classUnderTest.MyTestMethod(/* input parameters */);

           // Assert
           Assert.Equal(/* expected value */, result);
       }
   }
   ```

5. Run--------------------            AssertLastPosIsGreaterOrSame(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListCount, "@@@ last position of x in old array is greater than or equal to a position of x in the same array");

	    // assert Array.IndexOf(ArrayList.ToArray(), old_arrlist[old_arrList_Count-1]) equals new_arrList_IndexOf_x
            Assert.AreEqual(arrList.ToArray().IndexOf(arrList[arrList.Count - 1]), New_arrListIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  new positions of x in a old array are not changed
            AssertNoChangePosition(Old_arrListCount, Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, arrList.ToArray(), "@@@ new positions of x in a old array are not changed");
        }


        private void AssertLastPosIsGreaterOrSame(int oldPos, int count, string message)
        {
            Debug.Assert(oldPos >= count - 1, message);
        }

        private void AssertNoChangePosition(int oldCount, int oldIndex, int newIndex, int[] arrListArr, string message)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < oldCount; i++)
            {
                if (i != oldIndex)
                {
                    Debug.Assert(arrListArr[i] != arrListArr[newIndex], message);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Here are some important aspects of the code:

1. AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0); - This line ensures that the insert method won't be called with invalid index values.

2.  AssertLastPosIsGreaterOrSame - This method does the assertion for checking the last position of a value in an array has not been changed.

3. AssertNoChangePosition - This method checks if the positions of a value in an old array have not been changed when a new value is inserted.

